# Everything here is absent from the deployed image, and one entry is not a size
# question but a correctness one. Both reasons are stated per entry, because a
# line without a reason is a line that gets deleted by the next person who does
# not know what it was for.

# `.tmp` is the size that matters: 5.0 GB of other projects' checkouts, staged
# assets and vendored packs. `vercel deploy` uploads the DIRECTORY, not the git
# index, so gitignoring it does nothing here — only this file does. Without it a
# deploy is slow at best and over the upload limit at worst.
.tmp/

# Build output and installed dependencies. Vercel installs from pnpm-lock.yaml
# with the installCommand in vercel.json, so shipping a local node_modules is
# not only wasteful: a host-specific tree in the upload is a tree that disagrees
# with the lockfile the build is supposed to resolve from.
node_modules/
.next/
apps/web/.next/
apps/web/dist/
packages/*/dist/
packages/*/*/dist/

# The M0 stack, the local Compose file and the env template. Compose is the
# LOCAL development environment and is deliberately kept in the repo (section
# 37); it has no business in a production image.
compose*.yaml
.env
.env.example

# Native build output from the sibling Rust projects that share this directory,
# and the beads database — the tracker is source of truth on disk, not a build
# input, and shipping it would let a deploy write to it.
target/
.beads/

# Tooling directories that carry no runtime meaning.
.git/
.github/
docs/
scripts/
.vscode/
.idea/

# Test and gate artifacts. None of these are production inputs, and the gate
# scripts in scripts/ run in CI against the checkout, not in the deployment.
coverage/
*.log
.DS_Store

# The city art under apps/web/public/art IS required and is deliberately absent
# from this list. An earlier revision of this file excluded `apps/web/public/`
# wholesale, which would have shipped a Next.js app that renders a world with no
# sprites in it. `public/` must reach the deployment; the `dist/` entries above
# are the build output that shares the name, not the art.
